<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solapur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solapur is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. A fortified town held in turn by the Bahmani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> sultanates and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, it passed to the British in 1818. In the late 19th century it grew into a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textile manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solapur.jpg" alt="Solapur" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jadhavvv91 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solapur_Municipal_Corporation_building,_Indra_Bhuvan_credit_Alka_Kshirsagar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Solapur lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> in the south-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, at an average elevation of 458 metres. It is about 410 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, 245 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and 305 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, and it stands on a major highway and at a junction of railway lines between Mumbai, Pune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> and Hyderabad. The climate is dry, with three seasons, summer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and winter. The name is believed to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> words sola, sixteen, and pura, village: the present city was considered to spread over sixteen villages, among them Ahmedpur, Fatehpur and Sonallagi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was ruled by various dynasties, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a> and Bahamanis. Inscriptions from the time of the Kalachuris of Kalyani show the town was called Sonnalage, an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> name later pronounced Sonnalagi, and it was the main commercial hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription found at Kamati in Mohol gives the name Sonalipur, and inscriptions in the fort give Sonalpur and Sandalpur.</p><p>The Solapur district was formed in stages in the 19th century: in 1838 it became a sub-district of Ahmednagar, and in 1871 it was reformed with subdivisions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>. After the reorganisation of 1956 Solapur belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, and in 1960 it became a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><p>Weavers of the Padmashali community moved to Solapur and other places in search of work when imported English cloth undercut hand weaving. Many worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mills. The Manacha Shri Ajoba Ganpati temple began celebrating the Ganesh festival in 1885, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Lokmanya Tilak</a>, who visited Solapur, started the Ganesh festival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in 1893. During the independence movement the Solapur Municipal Council hoisted the Indian national flag on its building on 6 April 1930, in the spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s Dandi March.</p><p>Residents enjoyed full freedom from 9 to 11 May 1930. Four men, Mallappa Dhanshetti, Abdul Rasool Qurban Hussein, Jagannath Bhagwan Shinde and Shrikisan Laxminarayan Sarada, were hanged by the British on 12 January 1931 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, and the city became known as the City of Hutatmas, the City of Martyrs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Solapur is a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and Telugu cultural influences. It has long been a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/power-loom">power looms</a> and handlooms, and Solapuri chadars were the first product in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> to receive a Geographical Indication tag. It also leads Maharashtra in the production of beedi cigarettes. The chief deity of the city is Shri Shivyogi Siddheshwar, and the Gadda Yatra fair, with the Nandi-dhawaj procession at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makar-sankranti">Makar Sankranti</a>, is associated with his marriage. The Shri Siddheshwar Swami Temple and the Bhuikot Fort are its main sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rajas governed a dry Deccan territory of millet and cotton near <b>Solapur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/akalkot-state">Akalkot State</a></li></ul></section>
